            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Applies the Bessel function of the first kind of order 0, J0, to every\nelement of a tensor.\n\nIt is the shape of waves on anything circular - the ripples on a pond,\nthe way a round drum skin vibrates - and in FM synthesis J0 of the\nmodulation index is how loud the carrier stays.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nJ0 starts at 1 at x = 0 and then oscillates like a cosine whose swings\nslowly shrink (by about 1 over the square root of x). Its zeros are not\nevenly spaced at first - 2.405, 5.520, 8.654, 11.79 - but settle towards a\ngap of pi. It is symmetric: J0(-x) = J0(x).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.bessel_j0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it\ntriggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is\nthe same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device\nas the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nDefined for every real number; very large inputs give small values that\nkeep oscillating.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200\nevenly spaced values from its start to its stop (0 to 20 here), and the\nupper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is J0(x) of each\nof those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from 0 to 20,\ndrawn from -0.5 (bottom) to 1.05 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace\nto look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.bessel_j1 is the next order. t.special.spherical_bessel_j0\nis the spherical (3-D) version, sin(x) / x. t.special.i0 is the\n'modified' Bessel function - the same equation with the sign flipped,\nwhich grows instead of oscillating.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 720,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Applies the Bessel function of the first kind of order 0, J0, to every\nelement of a tensor.\n\nIt is the shape of waves on anything circular - the ripples on a pond,\nthe way a round drum skin vibrates - and in FM synthesis J0 of the\nmodulation index is how loud the carrier stays.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nJ0 starts at 1 at x = 0 and then oscillates like a cosine whose swings\nslowly shrink (by about 1 over the square root of x). Its zeros are not\nevenly spaced at first - 2.405, 5.520, 8.654, 11.79 - but settle towards a\ngap of pi. It is symmetric: J0(-x) = J0(x).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.bessel_j0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it\ntriggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is\nthe same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device\nas the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nDefined for every real number; very large inputs give small values that\nkeep oscillating.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200\nevenly spaced values from its start to its stop (0 to 20 here), and the\nupper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is J0(x) of each\nof those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from 0 to 20,\ndrawn from -0.5 (bottom) to 1.05 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace\nto look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.bessel_j1 is the next order. t.special.spherical_bessel_j0\nis the spherical (3-D) version, sin(x) / x. t.special.i0 is the\n'modified' Bessel function - the same equation with the sign flipped,\nwhich grows instead of oscillating."
        },
